WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:06.399
Interplanetary Transmissions: Stardust is
a new book of proceedings from the Santa Fe

00:00:06.399 --> 00:00:08.730
Institute's second interplanetary Festival.

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:12.160
Woven throughout this volume are original
writings from some of the visionary scientists

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:16.470
and futurists who spoke at the event and also
10 original works of art.

00:00:16.470 --> 00:00:20.260
These pieces consists of two or three meaningful
photographs woven together at the beginning

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:21.710
of each chapter.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:24.910
We thought about the possibility of weaving
photographs together when we realized that

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:29.060
the human art of textile was a thread that
ran through all of the conversations of the

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:30.180
festivals panels.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:34.920
So shortly after InterPlanetary wrapped up,
the Santa Fe Institute Press team, the InterPlanetary

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:39.739
team, and SFI's president David Krakauer convened
to figure out how we wanted to visually tether

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:43.540
all of these panels together while still maintaining
that energy.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.750
We started talking about what our favorite
elements of the festival were.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:50.980
And for a while, we were wrapped up in conversing
about Nicholas de Monchaux's lecture, which

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:54.059
was really about the book that he wrote called
Fashioning Apollo.

00:00:54.059 --> 00:00:57.820
Fashioning Apollo was about the history of
the making of the Apollo 11 moon suit.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.350
The suit in and of itself actually is a really
lovely example of complexity.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:03.370
It's 13 distinct layers.

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:06.960
Each layer is made out of a very specific
material that serves a very specific function,

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.380
but when all of those layers are arranged
in a certain order and stitched together that

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:16.020
new composite fabric serves one very crucial
function, which is keeping a human being alive

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:17.750
on the surface of the moon.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:21.930
And the most interesting thing about that
history is that those suits, these technological

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:28.030
innovations in wardrobe, these great new garments
were engineered actually by the seamstresses

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:29.150
at Playtex.

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:33.280
So the same hands that were making our bras
and our girdles and our pantyhose were the

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:36.490
hands that were responsible for this spectacular
suit.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:40.190
And that little morsel, that feminine bit
of the history of the Apollo 11 mission was

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:44.090
something that I didn't know about prior to
this interplanetary festival or prior to reading

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:45.909
that book and talking to Nick about it.

00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:48.490
So this was really yummy and we wanted to
keep it present.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:52.920
And when approached this project by virtue
of that conversation, David reminded us all

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:56.939
of the Fairchild semiconductor manufacturing
plant, which was right here in New Mexico

00:01:56.939 --> 00:01:59.979
in Shiprock, which is also Navajo nation.

00:01:59.979 --> 00:02:05.350
And that plant was predominantly employed
by grossly underpaid Navajo women, the thought

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:09.459
being at the time that because they have a
centuries long culture of weaving and textile

00:02:09.459 --> 00:02:17.849
artisanry, they could apply their nimble dexterity
to the manufacturing of semiconductor chips

00:02:17.849 --> 00:02:21.090
that would eventually be deployed in a variety
of new technologies.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.550
And from there, we started thinking about
computation in general and how actually the

00:02:24.550 --> 00:02:29.750
first computer, the computer from which all
of the computers we use today derived, was

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:30.750
really a loom.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:34.360
It was a machine that was made for the production
of textiles and the punch cards that allowed

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:39.200
for these very intricate designs to be incorporated
into the fabric were stolen and re-appropriated

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:42.780
and used in the very first versions of the
computers that got us to the Moon.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:47.390
And so we realized that there was this rich
foundation of textile, of craftsmanship, of

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:53.090
weaving and of handiwork that would essentially
be relegated to women's work.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:56.770
And that the women who produced these works
were responsible for the foundation upon which

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:59.440
all of our technological achievements depend.

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:03.830
And that was a really lovely thing that we
realized often goes unacknowledged and we

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:05.489
wanted to shed light upon it.

00:03:05.489 --> 00:03:10.690
So we started thinking about how to visually
keep that alive in the art for this book.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:14.300
From there, we all broke up and started brainstorming
about how to achieve this visualization.

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:16.890
And we went a million different directions.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:20.860
At first, we thought we might include images
of old punch cards from Jacquard looms.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:24.340
And then we thought we could even encode some
and share secret messages throughout the book

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:25.840
with our readers.

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:29.500
We thought about the fact that humans aren't
the only things on this earth that weave,

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.540
and it would be somewhat hubristic and anti-InterPlanetary.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:35.480
If we only included images of works created
by humans.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:39.870
So we thought about spiders and their webs,
and perhaps including imagery that relates

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:44.760
to both of those things, the weaver and the
woven item, in this book.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:48.600
But then we thought that this would be a fun
opportunity to collaborate with some contemporary

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.170
fiber artists.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:51.360
We could highlight their work.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.650
And we could also showcase exactly how the
medium of fiber art has evolved.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.940
We thought about working with a local Navajo
weaver named Melissa Cody.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:02.840
And then we thought about working with a fabulous
photographer named Kyle Myers who's using

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:06.540
textile and photography in a really clever
way together.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:11.269
And then we just started thinking about photography
in general, how again, this year was a celebration

00:04:11.269 --> 00:04:15.300
of how human beings walked on the moon 50
years ago.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:19.539
And now at InterPlanetary, we're all staring
up at the moon 50 years later, wondering what's

00:04:19.539 --> 00:04:20.539
to come.

00:04:20.539 --> 00:04:27.219
And we have a rich library of images from
then to now that are very inspiring in that

00:04:27.219 --> 00:04:28.219
way.

00:04:28.219 --> 00:04:32.009
So when we first experimented with weaving
photographs together, we learned that really

00:04:32.009 --> 00:04:36.479
colorful images suffer from this process,
that highly detailed images suffer from this

00:04:36.479 --> 00:04:37.479
process.

00:04:37.479 --> 00:04:42.249
So we started thinking about contrast and
void and composition, and we decided to take

00:04:42.249 --> 00:04:46.629
some visual components that were present in
each of the festival's panels and weave those

00:04:46.629 --> 00:04:52.900
together with the hope that the juxtaposition,
the emergent image would be this emblematic

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:56.639
image of the content that was discussed in
the panel writ large.

00:04:56.639 --> 00:05:00.330
We must've done 50 of these pieces before
we settled on the 10 or so that made it into

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:03.770
the book, but it was a really wonderful process.

00:05:03.770 --> 00:05:09.500
If our mission was to shed light on this side
of artistry and these invisible women, these

00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:16.310
artisans who are behind the technological
scenes, who paved the way for what we've accomplished,

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:21.389
it felt like the weaving itself was a, was
a practice in honoring that medium.

00:05:21.389 --> 00:05:25.009
I should also take the opportunity to say
that everyone on the InterPlanetary team and

00:05:25.009 --> 00:05:26.999
everyone on the Press team are women.

00:05:26.999 --> 00:05:31.000
So while we never explicitly addressed this,
I don't think it was lost on any of us.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:35.539
When we were sharing a space, talking about
the content of these panels and producing

00:05:35.539 --> 00:05:39.069
these pieces in this particular medium for
this book.

00:05:39.069 --> 00:05:43.559
So that was a really lovely embodiment of
the type of respect we hope our readers have

00:05:43.559 --> 00:05:46.520
for everything we were attempting to draw
attention to.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:47.999
And I'm really proud of the work that we did.

00:05:47.999 --> 00:05:49.819
I think these pieces are gorgeous.

00:05:49.819 --> 00:05:52.190
I'm so excited to include them in this volume.

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:52.639
And I hope that you enjoy them too.

